{"classes":["","diverted","regulated","reservoir"],"exclude":["diverted","reservoir"],"notes":{"diverted":"Hydro-diverted: most of the natural flow is taken out upstream, so the natural flood statistic overstates the flow.","regulated":"Flow controlled by a hydro scheme (storage dam, control gates or tailrace): natural flood statistics do not describe it.","reservoir":"Hydro lake or dam structure, not a river reach."},"schemes":[{"id":"clutha","name":"Clutha hydro scheme and lake controls","operator":"Contact Energy","note":"Clyde (Lake Dunstan) and Roxburgh are storage dams with the stations at the dam; the Clutha below is regulated. Lake Hawea is a controlled storage (Hawea River below the control dam). The Kawarau Falls gates (1926) at the Lake Wakatipu outlet do not control its outflow at normal flows. EA Report 4 (3.4, p. 20): \"while Lake Wakatipu has control gates at its outlet, these were built for mining purposes and are very seldom used. Therefore, only Lake Hāwea is controlled and outflows from the other two lakes are classed as tributary flows\"; its attributes table lists Lake Wakatipu as a run-of-river reservoir and Lake Hawea as controlled. The Electricity Commission's Lake Level History (Opus, 27 Feb 2009, 3.6.1 and Table 3.8, pp. 26-27; read in rf-6u2) counts Wakatipu among \"the natural Lakes Wanaka and Wakatipu\": the structure (1924-26) was built \"initially to close the outlet and allow access to gold deposits in the river bed\", its gates were used until 1938 and again in 1941-1956 for the construction of Roxburgh (an earlier note, from a search excerpt, had the gates holding floods back: the report says that of the Taupo gates, 2.1.1). take = 0 (an estimate of 0.3 until rf-6u2): the point marks nothing and stays listed for coverage.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydroelectric_power_in_New_Zealand","https://www.emi.ea.govt.nz/Environment/Datasets/HydrologicalModellingDataset/1_InfrastructureAndHydroConstraintAttributes/HMD%20Report%204%20-%20Hydro-power%20Schemes%20Background%20and%20Descriptions.pdf","https://www.emi.ea.govt.nz/Environment/Datasets/HydrologicalModellingDataset/1_InfrastructureAndHydroConstraintAttributes/20241231_InfrastructureAndHydroConstraintAttributes.csv","https://emidatasets.blob.core.windows.net/publicdata/Datasets/Environment/_AdditionalInformation/ArchivedDatasets/20100630_HydrologicalData/20090331_Lake_level_history/20090331_Lake_level_history_report.pdf"]},{"id":"roaring_meg","name":"Roaring Meg","operator":"Pioneer Energy","note":"Upper and Lower Roaring Meg stations take the stream by pipeline; it returns at the lower station by the Kawarau. Approx intake.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_power_stations_in_New_Zealand"]},{"id":"waipori","name":"Waipori","operator":"Manawa Energy (formerly Trustpower)","note":"Lake Mahinerangi (Mahinerangi Dam) feeds four stations down the Waipori gorge (1A, 2A, 3, 4) through tunnels and penstocks; the riverbed between the dam and Waipori 4 carries a residual flow.","src":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_power_stations_in_New_Zealand"]},{"id":"waitaki","name":"Waitaki hydro scheme","operator":"Meridian Energy; Tekapo A and B: Genesis Energy","note":"Lake Tekapo goes by canal to Tekapo B and Lake Pukaki; Pukaki and Ohau go by canals to Ohau A (Lake Ruataniwha) and Ohau B and C (Lake Benmore). The Tekapo, Pukaki and Ohau rivers below their control structures carry only residual flows down to Lake Benmore. Benmore, Aviemore and Waitaki are storage dams; the Waitaki below is regulated to the sea.","src":["https://www.meridianenergy.co.nz/power-stations/hydro/about-the-waitaki-hydro-scheme","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waitaki_River"]}],"r":[[9007,2,0,95],[9008,2,0,95],[9009,2,0,95],[9333,2,0,94],[9334,2,0,94],[9335,2,0,94],[9336,2,0,94],[9423,2,0,94],[9424,2,0,94],[9999,2,0,93],[10000,2,0,93],[10001,2,0,93],[10066,2,0,93],[10067,2,0,93],[10068,2,0,93],[10069,2,0,93],[10146,2,0,93],[10147,2,0,93],[10148,2,0,93],[102